    <fr:title text="Unambiguity via the Diagonal Being an Isomorphism">Unambiguity via the Diagonal Being an Isomorphism</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <fr:p><fr:link href="sss-001V.xml" title="Finite Unambiguity" addr="sss-001V" type="local">Finite unambiguity</fr:link> does not serve as an adequate definition of unambiguity that is equivalent to <fr:link href="sss-001T.xml" title="Unambiguity as Subterminality" addr="sss-001T" type="local">[sss-001T]</fr:link> and <fr:link href="sss-001U.xml" title="Unambiguity as Unique Map into Codomain" addr="sss-001U" type="local">[sss-001U]</fr:link>. However, the definition attempted via finite unambiguity can be refined to something that is equivalent to these.</fr:p>
    <fr:p>A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is unambiguous if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta  : A \vdash  A \And  A]]></fr:tex> is an isomorphism. Equivalently, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is unambiguous if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\pi _{1} : A \And  A \vdash  A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\pi _{2} : A \And  A \vdash  A]]></fr:tex> are equal.</fr:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
